// src/app/data/private-ca-faq.data.ts
var PRIVATE_CA_FAQS = [
  [
    "What is a private CA?",
    "A certificate authority that only your organisation trusts. It has its own root and one or more issuing CAs, and it issues certificates for internal servers, users and devices. Because its root is not in public browser or operating-system trust stores, its certificates are trusted only by devices where you install that root."
  ],
  [
    "How is a private certificate different from a public SSL certificate?",
    "A public certificate chains to a root that every browser trusts, so it must follow CA/Browser Forum rules: registered domain names only, validated ownership and a maximum lifetime that is now 200 days. A private certificate follows your own policy \u2014 internal names, private IPs, custom key usages and longer lifetimes are all allowed \u2014 but only your managed devices trust it."
  ],
  [
    "Why won't browsers outside our company trust it?",
    "That is by design. Your root is distributed only to your own devices through Group Policy, Intune or MDM. Anything public-facing \u2014 your website, customer portals, public APIs \u2014 should keep using a publicly trusted certificate. The decider on this page shows which systems belong where."
  ],
  [
    "Can't we just use self-signed certificates?",
    "Self-signed certificates have no common root, no revocation and no lifecycle tracking, so users learn to click through warnings and attackers can impersonate internal services. A private CA gives every internal certificate one trusted chain, CRL and OCSP revocation, and automatic renewal."
  ],
  [
    "We already run Microsoft AD CS. Do we have to replace it?",
    "No. A hosted management platform can sit over AD CS so Microsoft and non-Microsoft certificates live in one inventory with the same policies and alerts. When you are ready, we plan a phased migration to the hosted private CA, template by template."
  ],
  [
    "Where is the root key kept?",
    "In a hardware security module operated by the vendor, behind layered, multi-person access controls, and kept offline except to sign issuing CAs. On the Dedicated edition the HSM is single-tenant; where the platform supports on-prem, it can sit in your own data centre."
  ],
  [
    "Which enrolment methods are supported?",
    "ACME for servers and Kubernetes, SCEP for phones and network gear (often via Intune or another MDM), EST for devices and Linux, Windows auto-enrolment through AD, a REST API for your own apps, and manual CSR upload. Exact connectors depend on the platform; we confirm them for your stack."
  ],
  [
    "How do we get every device to trust our root?",
    "Windows devices receive it through Group Policy or Intune, Macs and phones through an MDM profile, and Linux servers through configuration management. We prepare the packages and verify coverage before the first certificate goes live."
  ]
];

// src/app/data/iot-pki-faq.data.ts
var IOT_PKI_FAQS = [
  [
    "What is IoT PKI?",
    "A public key infrastructure built for connected devices: a private certificate authority, HSM-protected keys and the tooling to issue a unique X.509 certificate to every device, then renew, revoke and update those certificates over the device's life."
  ],
  [
    'What is a device "birth certificate"?',
    "The first certificate a device receives, usually during manufacturing. It binds the device's serial number to a key pair generated on the device. Many fleets later swap it for a shorter-lived operational certificate after onboarding."
  ],
  [
    "Why not use passwords or API tokens on devices?",
    "They are usually shared across a batch, stored in firmware and easy to extract. One leak exposes the whole fleet. A per-device private key in a secure element can't be copied to another unit, so a breach stays contained to one device."
  ],
  [
    "Where can certificates be installed?",
    "On the silicon by the chip supplier, on your production line through an injection station, or remotely in the field over EST, SCEP or CMPv2. The right point depends on your volumes, line speed and the secure hardware on the device."
  ],
  [
    "Which protocols do you support?",
    "EST, SCEP and CMPv2 for device enrolment and renewal, ACME for Linux-class gateways, and a REST API for factory and cloud systems. Devices then use their certificates for mutual TLS \u2014 for example to an MQTT broker, AWS IoT Core or Azure IoT Hub."
  ],
  [
    "DigiCert or Sectigo \u2014 which should we choose?",
    "Both offer IoT PKI with private CAs and extra device protections. The choice usually comes down to scale, hosting model, existing CA relationships and integrations. We run a short requirements session and a side-by-side, then recommend one."
  ],
  [
    "How long should device certificates last?",
    "Birth certificates often match the product's service life \u2014 10 to 20 years \u2014 because a device may sit offline for long periods. Operational certificates are typically shorter and renewed automatically. We plan key sizes, algorithms and the CA hierarchy so the root outlives the fleet."
  ],
  [
    'What does "quantum-ready" mean for devices?',
    "Long-life devices shipped today may still be in service when quantum computers threaten RSA and ECC. Crypto-agile profiles, firmware that can accept new trust anchors and a plan for NIST's post-quantum algorithms (such as ML-DSA) let you move later without recalling hardware. Confirm current PQC support with the vendor."
  ]
];

// src/app/data/iot-device-certificates-faq.data.ts
var IOT_DEVICE_CERTIFICATES_FAQS = [
  [
    "What is an IoT device certificate?",
    "An X.509 digital certificate issued to one device. It binds the device's identity \u2014 usually its serial number \u2014 to a public key whose private half stays on the device. The device uses it to prove who it is, typically over mutual TLS, and to sign or encrypt data."
  ],
  [
    "What's the difference between a birth and an operational certificate?",
    "A birth certificate is written during manufacturing and lasts the product's life; it proves the device is genuine. An operational certificate is issued after onboarding, is scoped to your cloud or customer, and is renewed every one to five years. Many fleets use both."
  ],
  [
    "How is the price per device worked out?",
    "The yearly rate depends on fleet size: \u20B919 per device up to 10,000 devices, \u20B912 up to 1 lakh and \u20B97 above that, applied to every device. A 10-year long-life certificate is \u20B999 per device, once. Integration is \u20B91,49,999 one-time. All indicative and excluding 18% GST."
  ],
  [
    "Which protocols can devices use to get certificates?",
    "EST and SCEP for most devices, CMPv2 for industrial equipment, ACME for Linux-class gateways, and a REST API for factory systems. We recommend one per device class \u2014 the profile builder above shows our default."
  ],
  [
    "Do these certificates work with AWS IoT Core and Azure IoT Hub?",
    "Yes. AWS IoT Core and Azure IoT Hub (through the Device Provisioning Service) both accept devices that authenticate with X.509 certificates from your own CA. We register the CA, set up enrolment groups or just-in-time provisioning, and test the mutual-TLS connection."
  ],
  [
    "ECC or RSA keys \u2014 which should we use?",
    "ECC (P-256) is the usual choice for constrained devices: smaller certificates, faster handshakes and less power. RSA-2048 still suits older stacks. Long-life certificates may use P-384. The platform supports both, with SHA-2 or SHA-3 signatures."
  ],
  [
    "What happens when a device is lost, cloned or retired?",
    "We revoke its certificate. Brokers that check OCSP or CRLs \u2014 or your device registry \u2014 then refuse it, while the rest of the fleet keeps working. The record stays for audit."
  ],
  [
    "Can devices already in the field get certificates?",
    "Often, yes. If a device can reach an enrolment endpoint and has a bootstrap credential, it can enrol remotely over EST or SCEP. We review the firmware and connectivity first."
  ]
];

// src/app/data/cloud-code-signing-faq.data.ts
var CLOUD_CODE_SIGNING_FAQS = [
  [
    "What is cloud code signing?",
    "It is code signing delivered as a service. Your code-signing certificate's private key is generated and kept in a certified cloud HSM, and your developers and build pipelines sign through an API under the rules you set \u2014 instead of plugging in a USB token."
  ],
  [
    "Does the private key ever leave the HSM?",
    "No. Your build computes a hash of the file and sends only that hash. The signature is created inside the HSM and returned. Nobody \u2014 including XcellHost \u2014 can export the key."
  ],
  [
    "Does it meet the June 2023 hardware key requirement?",
    "Yes. Since June 2023 the CA/Browser Forum has required code-signing keys to be generated and stored on hardware certified to FIPS 140-2 Level 2 or Common Criteria EAL4+. The cloud HSMs used by these services meet that bar."
  ],
  [
    "What changes in February 2026?",
    "New code-signing certificates are limited to one year of validity. HSM-based plans can be bought for longer terms but the certificate is reissued every year. On a cloud signing service we schedule and complete that reissue for you, so pipelines keep working."
  ],
  [
    "Which CI/CD tools can sign this way?",
    "GitHub Actions, GitLab CI, Jenkins, Azure DevOps and Bitbucket, plus build tools such as Gradle, Maven and ANT. Anything that can run a command line and keep a secret can usually be connected; we set it up and document it."
  ],
  [
    "Do I need OV or EV?",
    "OV (organisation-validated) suits most software and is also available to individual developers. EV is for registered organisations only, has the strictest vetting and is required for kernel-mode drivers submitted through Microsoft's Hardware Dev Center. Our Team edition uses OV; Business and Enterprise include EV."
  ],
  [
    "Does EV still remove SmartScreen warnings instantly?",
    "Not any more. Since March 2024 Microsoft SmartScreen builds reputation for EV-signed files the same way it does for OV \u2014 through clean downloads over time. EV still gives the highest assurance of who the publisher is, and signing consistently with the same certificate helps reputation build."
  ],
  [
    "Can we limit when and by whom signing happens?",
    "Yes. You can restrict each key to named users or services, require one or two approvers with MFA, allow signing only from protected branches and only inside release windows. Try the policy builder above to see how a request is checked."
  ]
];

